#
# prima-materia — the Mandaean transmission, taken down from the register
#
# The instance was registered in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl and stands
# here now that passages have been read. It was taken out of the register for
# the reason SPEC §14 gives first: this is a transmission that prices its own
# ritual faults. It states, case by case and in numbers, what a performance
# costs when something goes wrong in it, and it states which faults make the
# performance void rather than merely expensive. Very little else in this
# holding does that; most of what the graph carries names a condition and
# leaves the consequence of breaking it unsaid.
#
# Order of the file: the transmission and what was read; the works as
# identifiers; the consecration and the faults that void it; the tariff; the
# counted period that keeps being re-begun; the consecration whose validity
# hangs on a third person's death; the calendar; the fallen priest; the two
# places where the scripture denies the efficacy of its own possession; the
# watch-houses and what is handed over at them; the cautions; and the dispute
# about women priests, which is the one place where the works read here
# contradict each other outright.
#

pmt:Mandaean a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "Mandaean transmission"@en, "Mandäische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A living gnostic transmission with its own scripture and priesthood, teaching an ascent of the soul past stations of examination."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-09-20. Three works were opened. First, the Ginzā in the German of Mark Lidzbarski of 1925, read closely at the right side, book one, the commandments; book two, first piece, the parallel commandments; book five, third piece, the journey past the watch-houses, in full; book thirteen, the priest's prayer and the warnings; book fifteen, first and seventh pieces. Second, the Qolastā in the English of E. S. Drower of 1959, searched over the whole and read at the baptism and masiqta prayers and their rubrics. Third, Drower's field monograph of 1937, read closely at chapters nine and ten, the consecration of the tarmida and of the ganzibra, in full, and at the passages on the insignia, on the baptism of children, on marriage, and on the calendar."@en ;
    skos:note "Not read: the left Ginzā and the greater part of the right; the Drāšā d-Yahia, which was not opened in this run although Lidzbarski's German of 1915 is public domain; the burial and ritual-meal chapters of the field monograph. Not obtainable: the Alf Trisar Šuialia, the priestly manual in which the fault rules of this transmission actually stand. Its edition of 1960 exists only in print and in a lending copy whose text file answers with 401, and the transliteration held by the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon refuses automated retrieval. That is a property of the work and not of this run, and it is the reason the tariff below is entered from the field record rather than from the manual."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays at disputed and this run did not settle it, but the shape of the dispute is worth stating, because the received summary of it is not the one the material supports. The question is usually put as west against east, a Palestinian origin against a Mesopotamian one. What actually divides the scholarship is a question about evidence: no manuscript of this corpus is older than the sixteenth century, and everything claimed for an earlier state of the text rests on the copyist chains named in the colophons. Whoever holds those chains to be reliable dates the transmission to the second or third century; whoever holds them to be constructions follows the late dating. A reader of this file should know that the works read here do not decide it and that no node below claims to."@en ;
    skos:note "What kind of witness each work is, since they are not the same kind. The Ginzā and the Qolastā are transmitted books and are cited as such. The monograph of 1937 is the first fixation in writing of rules which its author was told by priests and of proceedings she attended; its passages are entered with pm:fieldAttestation and the gathering is named at each node. Where she quotes the priestly manual, the claim is second-hand and says so. The three are not interchangeable, and the file does not let the field record stand in for the scripture: the searches at examinations/searches-mandaean.ttl were carried precisely to find out which of the rules below the scripture itself states, and the answer is none of them."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ginzā Rabbā; Drāšā d-Yahia; Qolastā" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:GinzaRabba, pmw:DrasaDYahia, pmw:Qolasta, pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,1-320,16), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das große Buch der Mandäer (Göttingen: Vandenhoeck und Ruprecht, 1925), pp. 322-324" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:GinzaRabba a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ginzā Rabbā, the Great Treasure"@en, "Ginzā Rabbā, der große Schatz"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-20 in the German of Mark Lidzbarski of 1925, which is the translation the scholarship cites and which carries the page and line of Petermann's Mandaic text of 1867 in its running heads. Every source string below therefore gives the Petermann reference and the page of the German, because the two count differently and a later reader needs both. Of the two the Petermann reference is the safer: the copy read here is a scan whose optical reading damages the page numerals in the running heads, turning twenty-two into ninety-two and one hundred and eighty-four into a brace and eighty-four, while the text references beside them survive. The page numbers below were reconstructed from the sequence of the heads and not read off single ones."@en .

pmw:DrasaDYahia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Drāšā d-Yahia, the Book of John"@en, "Drāšā d-Yahia, das Johannesbuch"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. Lidzbarski's German of 1915 is public domain and its chapter of admonitions is reported to carry the same prohibition of sorcery that the Ginzā carries, which would make it a second witness inside the corpus for pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam. Not opening it was a decision of this run and not a property of the work."@en .

pmw:Qolasta a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Qolastā, the canonical prayerbook"@en, "Qolastā, das kanonische Gebetbuch"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-20 in the English of E. S. Drower of 1959, the edition that numbers the prayers from one to four hundred and fourteen. It was opened for one question and searched over the whole: whether the liturgical book itself anywhere states that a performance is void or is to be repeated. It does not. See pmp:MandaeanQolastaIrritumSearch."@en .

pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Alf Trisar Šuialia, the Thousand and Twelve Questions"@en, "Alf Trisar Šuialia, die tausendundzwölf Fragen"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and not obtainable in this run. It is the priestly manual, and one of its parts carries the title Mhita u-Asuta, blow and healing, that is, ritual faults and their remedy. Drower's edition of 1960 exists in print; the lending scan answers 401 to its text file and the transliteration at the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon refuses automated retrieval. Two claims below are entered second-hand through the field monograph because of this, and they are the two that would be worth checking first once a copy is at hand."@en .

pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)"@en, "Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (1937)"@de ;
    skos:note "Not a work of this corpus and deliberately not listed under pm:corpusWork. It is the field record: the first fixation in writing of rules the recorder was told by priests and of proceedings she attended in Iraq between the nineteen-twenties and 1937. It is named here because the nodes that rest on it must be able to say which work their source lies in, and because a reader who sees the tariff below should be able to tell at a glance that it comes from this book and not from the scripture."@en .

#
# The consecration, and the faults that make it void
#

pmp:MandaeanTarmidaTarasa a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The setting up of the crown: consecration of a tarmida"@en, "Das Aufsetzen der Krone: die Weihe eines Tarmida"@de ;
    skos:definition "The consecration of a priest of the first degree, which the transmission calls the setting up of the crown on him. Seven priests at least and a ganzibra baptize one another; a sheep is killed beside the water as a ransom for the novice; the assembled priests examine his body to establish that it is without blemish; a reed hut is built and the novice recites the whole Book of Souls from memory while the priests follow with open copies to see that no mistake is made. He and his master keep vigil in the hut through the night. At dawn the hut is pulled down and he enters a second hut, where he is invested with ring, crowns, wreath and staff and remains a week without sleeping. Sixty days of purity follow, in which he lives apart, prepares his own food, immerses three times a day and receives three secret words each day. At the end he celebrates a masiqta alone and by heart in the name of the ganzibra who taught him."@en ;
    skos:note "The recital by heart is a condition and not a display: a novice must be word perfect in the Book of Souls, in the whole ritual of the masiqta and in the rahmi before he is considered worthy of priesthood, and at the masiqta the ganzibra de-consecrates his own dress so as to become a spectator and stands by holding the text in his hand to prompt his pupil if he stumbles. The transmission builds its own error protection into the rite, and it does so at the one point where the candidate is alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in English in 1937. She states at this place that she had not witnessed the ceremonies herself but had several informants who related them in great detail and whose descriptions tallied; the Mandaic terms are carried in her transliteration beside the English. The rite as she gives it is therefore reported speech from named and unnamed priests and not observation, and she says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran: Their Cults, Customs, Magic, Legends, and Folklore (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 146-165" .

pmc:MandaeanAndirunaLibriDesunt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "Two scrolls absent, and the proceedings are void"@en, "Zwei Rollen fehlen, und die Handlung ist ungültig"@de ;
    skos:definition "All the sacred books that can be found must be placed in the reed hut in which the novice and his master keep vigil, and two of them in particular, the Diwan Alma Rishaya and the Diwan Malkutha Ileytha. If these are not present, the proceedings are invalid."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the form and not the content. The condition is an observable state of the room, it is stated in advance, and the consequence named for failing it is not a penalty but voidness: the rite does not become expensive, it does not count. A holding that wants to know which transmissions can be shown to have failed needs the cases where the transmission itself says what would make a performance no performance."@en ;
    skos:note "The books are named and the reason they are named is not given. Whether they are required because they are read from, because they are the warrant of the office, or for some third reason, the field record does not say, and no node here supplies it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her account of the consecration as related to her by priestly informants; no speaker is named at this place."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 153" .

pmc:MandaeanUxorisTamia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The wife becomes unclean and the consecration is void"@en, "Die Frau wird unrein, und die Weihe ist ungültig"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the wife of a married candidate menstruates, miscarries or gives birth during his consecration, the resulting pollution invalidates the consecration and everyone concerned in it, beginning with three hundred and sixty-six baptisms for the officiating ganzibra. If it is the wife of the ganzibra who becomes unclean during the rite, he incurs the same three hundred and sixty-six baptisms and is in addition debarred for life from ever becoming rišh ʿama."@en ;
    skos:note "The event that voids the rite is involuntary, it happens to a person who is not performing it, and it is not in the knowledge or the power of anyone who is. The cost falls entirely on those performing. No relief is offered on the ground that they could not have known or prevented it, and the transmission does offer such relief elsewhere, to a child: see pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia, where the very same argument is made and made about somebody else."@en ;
    skos:note "Two prices in one sentence and they are of different kinds. The baptisms are a count that can be worked through, and pmc:MandaeanBaptismataMultiplicata shows how; the bar on the highest office is permanent and attaches to the accident and not to any act."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants in her account of the consecration; no speaker is named at this place and the rule is given as a rule and not as a case."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 148" .

pmc:MandaeanTaghaLapsus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "Should the crown fall, he is a novice again"@en, "Fällt die Krone, ist er wieder Novize"@de ;
    skos:definition "The tagha, the crown worn beneath the turban, is the chief part of the priest's insignia and the consecration is spoken of as the setting up of the crown on him. Should the crown fall accidentally from the head or from the hand of a priest while he is officiating, he becomes a novice and must undergo a form of reconsecration."@en ;
    skos:note "An office lost by an accident of gravity, with no fault, no intention and no further condition. It is the purest case in this file of the transmission attaching a consequence to an observable physical event rather than to a state of the person, and the consequence is the largest it has: not a penalty but the loss of the rank itself."@en ;
    skos:note "The same image carries the same meaning in the scripture, in a place this run did not read at the wording and therefore does not enter: Lidzbarski quotes, in his notes to the Book of John, a passage saying of proud priests and bishops that the crown has fallen from their head. Whether that is the same formula in the same sense is a question for whoever opens the Drāšā d-Yahia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on ritual dress and insignia; the rule is given as current practice, and the sentence of the Alf Trisar Šuialia about the crown and kingliness being set on the head stands beside it as her quotation from the manual."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 34" .

pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlfTrisarSuialia ;
    rdfs:label "The sight of the priest must be faultless"@en, "Das Augenlicht des Priesters muss fehlerlos sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition the priestly manual sets on the body of a priest, as quoted in the field record: according to the Alf Trisar Šuialia the priest's sight must be faultless. The surrounding rule of current practice is that the body must be pure, sound and perfect, that a circumcised man, an impotent man and a eunuch are debarred, and that a priest already ordained who takes an injury destroying his manhood or costing him a limb can no longer officiate."@en ;
    skos:note "The intermediary is named as a literal and not as the work identifier this file otherwise uses, because pm:readVia is declared as a literal and its value shape rejects anything else. That is a gap in the vocabulary and not a decision of this node: the work identifiers introduced on 2026-09-18 exist so that two files naming the same work find each other without a string comparison, and a node read through an intermediary is exactly the case where a later reader wants to ask which works the holding has read through. It is filed as prima-materia#759."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered second-hand and marked as such, because the manual could not be obtained; the surrounding practice is field-recorded and stands at the source string beside the manual. The reason for keeping the two apart is that they disagree: the recorder notes that the loss of an eye is apparently not now counted, while the manual she quotes requires faultless sight. A later reader who conflates them will read a rule as current that the field record reports as lapsed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Alf Trisar Šuialia, on the qualifications of a priest, quoted in E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147" .

#
# The tariff
#

pmc:MandaeanTaxatioRerum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The price list for a broken cult object"@en, "Die Preisliste für ein zerbrochenes Kultgerät"@de ;
    skos:definition "The counted penalties attaching to accidents to cult objects during the week in the hut and the sixty days of purity that follow it. If the clay table breaks, the novice incurs sixty baptisms and his master the same; if the fire-basin, both must be baptized by five priests; if the incense-holder, by three; if the staff floats away or breaks, both have fifty; if the ring is lost, fifty; if the crown, sixty; if the trousers, turban, girdle or stole are injured or lost, the novice and the ganzibra must receive a triple baptism from three priests."@en ;
    skos:note "Seven items and five different prices, and the price varies with which object was damaged rather than with how it happened or whose hand was on it. The transmission is doing something unusual here: it treats a ritual fault as a quantity, and it fixes the quantity by a property of the thing rather than by a property of the act. The graph should record that, because it is the precondition of the question a later reader will want to ask, namely whether the numbers stand in any relation to each other at all."@en ;
    skos:note "What the field record does not say, and it would be the first thing to look for in the priestly manual: what happens if the penalty is not paid. The numbers are stated; the consequence of leaving them unpaid is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the list is given as a list, without a case attached to any item of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 154" .

pmc:MandaeanTaxatioPollutionis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The same involuntary event, priced by the day it falls on"@en, "Dasselbe unwillkürliche Ereignis, nach dem Tag seines Eintritts berechnet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The gravest contamination during the week in the hut is nocturnal emission, and for that reason the candidate is not allowed to sleep for the whole week and a perpetual din is kept up to prevent him falling asleep. If it happens nevertheless, the price is fixed by the day: on the first three days and nights the novice and his master incur three hundred and sixty-six baptisms, on the fourth sixty, on the fifth fifty, on the sixth a triple baptism by three priests and the recitation of twenty-one rahmis. If it happens before the crowns have been put on him, the whole consecration is postponed for a year."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage the file was assembled around. One and the same event, involuntary and unobservable by anyone but the sleeper, costs five different amounts according to the stage the rite has reached, and the largest consequence is not the largest number but the one attached to the earliest point: before the investiture there is no price at all, only a year's delay. The transmission is not grading the fault by its gravity. It is grading it by how far the procedure had got."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated so that it is not read as more than it is: the graded scale is the recorder's report of what priests told her, it is given without a case, and the manual in which it presumably stands could not be obtained. What would settle whether the grading is a rule or a recollection is the Mhita u-Asuta section of the Alf Trisar Šuialia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; she notes in the same paragraph that if the candidate nods a little latitude is given, which is her own observation of how the rule is handled rather than part of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 154" .

pmc:MandaeanVigiliaSeptemDierum a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The consecration presupposes a week without sleep"@en, "Die Weihe setzt eine Woche ohne Schlaf voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MandaeanTarmidaTarasa ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MandaeanTaxatioPollutionis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The consequence is stated as a graded count of baptisms and, at the earliest stage, as postponement of the whole consecration for a year. The sleeplessness is not asceticism and the record says so: it is prescribed because of what sleep may bring, and the household arranges itself around it, beating drums, singing and entertaining guests all night so that the candidate shall not doze."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the same rule is reported again, with three days instead of seven, for the candidate for the high priesthood."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    skos:note "Entered as a prerequisite rather than as a caution because the transmission names what follows from failing it, and names it in numbers. What it does not name is any sign by which the failure would be known to anyone but the candidate, which makes the whole apparatus rest on his own report."@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 154 and 172" .

pmc:MandaeanBaptismataMultiplicata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The count is discharged by multiplying the celebrants"@en, "Die Zahl wird durch Vervielfachung der Vollzieher abgetragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "How a tariff of many baptisms is actually worked off. In the purification the recorder attended, each act of the baptism was received from three priests in turn, so that every action was performed nine times and the whole operation was repeated at noon and before sunset, giving the defiled priest nine baptisms in one day. A polluted ganzibra was served by seven priests and got through sixty-three baptisms in a day; performed by one priest once daily they would have taken a whole year, during which he would have been excommunicate."@en ;
    skos:note "The number is the thing, and the time is not. A penalty stated as a count of repetitions turns out to be payable in a day by adding celebrants, and the transmission does this openly and by arrangement rather than as an evasion. Whoever wants to read the counts in this file as a measure of severity has to reckon with the fact that the transmission itself treats them as divisible."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the one place in the file where the recorder writes as an eyewitness of a penalty being paid, and the count she gives is arithmetic she did herself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Observed by E. S. Drower at Litlata in 1934, at the beginning of the purification of an assistant priest, and published in English in 1937; the ganzibra's figure is reported to her rather than seen."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 175-176" .

#
# The counted period that is re-begun
#

pmc:MandaeanDiesVacuiRepetiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The sixty days are rarely accomplished in sixty days"@en, "Die sechzig Tage werden selten in sechzig Tagen vollendet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule governing the period of purity after the week in the hut. The chance visit or touch of a person or thing in a state of uncleanness may render that day void, and another day with the devotions that should have been performed in it is added at the end of the sixty; thus, the record says, the sixty days of purity are rarely accomplished in sixty days. For the candidate to the high priesthood the corresponding period is forty-five days, called the period assigned for a passage through the purgatories, and there the rule is harder: if pollution occurs in spite of precaution, the seclusion must be begun again."@en ;
    skos:note "Two devices for the same problem and they are not equally strong. A voided day is replaced; a voided seclusion is restarted. In both, a count that the transmission itself fixed is prevented from falling due by an event outside the candidate's control, and in both the transmission provides for that in advance rather than meeting it when it comes."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue is worth stating and is not identity. That generalization is about a count at which an effect is to arrive; here the count is a term to be served, and the effect at the end of it, the priesthood, is established by other means. What the two share is the provision made in advance for the count not completing, and a run that wants to widen the generalization to terms as well as thresholds should start here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; the remark that the sixty days are rarely accomplished in sixty is her own and follows the rule in the same sentence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 155-156 and 173" .

#
# The consecration that waits for a death
#

pmc:MandaeanIngirthaNafaqa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The message carried by a dying man"@en, "Die Botschaft, die ein Sterbender trägt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition on which a priest may be raised to the high priesthood. It can take place only when some pious aged person of ritually pure and priestly stock, married and not childless, is near death. That person is brought to the cult enclosure, washed, dressed in the death garment, and given a sealed phial of oil in the pocket of the shirt; the candidate says to him, I have brought it to thee, and bear thou it to Abathur; and the dying person is thereby purified of all past transgressions and passes into the worlds of light without a sojourn in the watch-houses."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims run together in one rite and the graph should keep them apart. One is about the candidate: his office depends on a death. The other is about the dying man: carrying the message exempts him from the stations the soul otherwise passes. The second is the only exemption from the watch-houses this file carries, and it is bought by dying at the right moment for somebody else's advancement."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder adds, without comment, that the shock of the cold water and the excitement and exertion usually hasten the end."@en ;
    skos:note "One condition of the rite is stated and then relaxed in the same sentence, which is worth keeping because the rest of this file is so exact: the consecration must be performed by a ganzibra, two priests and two acolytes, and in cases of dire necessity one priest has sufficed. The transmission that prices a broken incense-holder at a baptism by three priests also knows how to make an exception, and says so without a tariff attached."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants and in part from her own presence at Mandaean rites of the period; the formula is given in her transliteration with the priests' own English rendering beside it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 169-171" .

pmc:MandaeanIngirthaConvalescentia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "If the dying man recovers, the consecration is void"@en, "Genest der Sterbende, ist die Weihe ungültig"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the transmission says about the case in which the rite does not come off. It happens, rarely, that the person thought to be dying recovers. In that case a masiqta is read in his name, but the ceremonies as far as the candidate to the high priesthood is concerned are invalid, and he must wait until another suitable person is near death."@en ;
    skos:note "Both sides of the criterion are named, and the criterion is an event no party to the rite controls. The death makes the consecration; the recovery unmakes it, and the transmission says so in the same breath rather than finding the candidate at fault or providing a repair. That is the shape pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls describes, and this is the first Mandaean witness for it."@en ;
    skos:note "What keeps it from being a clean experiment, and it should be said at the node rather than left for a reader to notice: the parties are not indifferent to the outcome, the dying person is handled in ways the recorder says hasten the end, and the recorder also notes that Mandaeans are acute at recognizing the signs of imminent death. The event is uncontrolled; the selection of the case is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, from priestly informants; she reports the case as one that occurs rarely rather than as one she saw."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 171" .

#
# The calendar
#

pmc:MandaeanDiesMbattal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The days that are mbaṭṭal, useless"@en, "Die Tage, die mbaṭṭal sind, unnütz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Days on which no undertaking is to be begun and no religious ceremony performed, because the day is mbaṭṭal, useless and inauspicious: the twenty-second of the month, the twenty-fifth of Nuna, the first four days of Taura, and the day following the Panja festival. If a man dies on a mbaṭṭal day, a blessed oblation must be performed for him on a substitute at Panja."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is a date and nothing else. It is not a state of the person operating, not a state of the person operated on, and not a condition that anyone could meet by preparing better; and the term the transmission uses for the day is the same word it would use for a ritual that did not work. The repair, for the one case where the timing cannot be chosen, is performance at the right season upon a stand-in."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a witness for generalizations/efficacy-bound-to-the-calendar.ttl, where it is the eighth and the first from this transmission and the first from outside the Chinese, European and Graeco-Egyptian material that carries the other seven."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on cosmogony, astrology and holy-days; the rule is given as current observance, and she notes in her marriage chapter that a wedding was split across two days so as to avoid the day after Panja."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 88, with the marriage case at p. 60" .

pmc:MandaeanHamraEodemDie a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The priest must prepare the hamra the same day, or it is useless"@en, "Der Priester muss das Hamra am selben Tag bereiten, sonst ist es unnütz"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule governing the sacramental fluid made by kneading white raisins or grapes and dates into water: the priest must prepare the hamra the same day, or it is useless."@en ;
    skos:note "A term of hours attached to a substance, with the consequence stated in the same word the transmission uses of a dead day. It is the smallest falsifiable claim in this file and the cheapest to check, and no sign is given by which a useless hamra would be told from a good one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her account of a wedding she attended, where the preparation is described item by item as she saw it laid out."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 68" .

#
# The fallen priest
#

pmc:MandaeanPaisaq a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The priest who married a woman who was not a virgin"@en, "Der Priester, der eine Nichtjungfrau getraut hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "Should a priest assist at the marriage of a woman who is not a virgin, he is debarred from all his duties until he has received fifty full baptisms in new garments, and the ganzibra who officiated must go through three hundred and sixty before he may return to his office. The record names two men who went through this expiation. A priest so reduced is called paisaq and may thereafter perform no ceremony except the marriage of women who are not virgins, and the children of such a union are excluded from the priesthood for three generations."@en ;
    skos:note "The fact that triggers all of this is not known to the priest and is not his to establish: matrons examine the bride, and the record says in the same sentence that they sometimes make a mistake about her virginity. The penalty is nevertheless his, and theirs is not mentioned. It is the second of the three passages in this file where relief on the ground of not knowing is available in principle and is not extended to the officiant."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence outlasts the man by three generations and attaches to children who did nothing. Entered beside generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl for that reason, although the node is not used there: the cost to the priest is the tariff, and the cost to the descendants is a separate and permanent one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937; she names Shaikh Dukhayil and Shaikh Rumi as having gone through the expiation, and gives the term paisaq with its definition in her own glossary."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 175, with the exclusion of the children at p. 61" .

pmc:MandaeanSacerdosRitualiterMortuus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The defiled priest is treated as ritually dead"@en, "Der befleckte Priester gilt als rituell tot"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the purification of a defiled priest opened with, as the recorder saw it at Litlata in 1934. A blessed oblation was read in his name by a ganzibra and two priests; he wore an entirely new garment, held a new staff, and stayed mute while his three colleagues ate for the dead in his name, for he is considered ritually dead; and he sat facing south instead of north. The baptism followed, and when fifty baptisms had been performed upon him a second oblation was read over him and his purification was complete."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission does not repair the fault, it kills and restores the man. Two marks make the death a formal state and not a metaphor: the meal for the dead is eaten in his name by others, and he is seated at the orientation of the dead. The completion of the count is the only thing that ends it, and there is no sign attached to that completion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Observed by E. S. Drower at Litlata in 1934 and published in English in 1937; she was present at the beginning of the purification and describes the rite step by step as she watched it, with the Mandaic terms in her transliteration."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 175" .

pmc:MandaeanInfansSineScientia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "The child sinned unwittingly, and the priest pays"@en, "Das Kind sündigte unwissentlich, und der Priester zahlt"@de ;
    skos:definition "If during the baptism of an infant the child soils or wets its clothing, the priest continues the ceremony as though nothing had happened, but must afterwards go through a complete baptism at the hands of a ganzibra and a priest. The child's baptism is not invalidated, because it sinned unwittingly, and if it dies it is entitled to full burial rites; if it lives and reaches an age of decent behaviour it atones by being baptized in a new garment at the appointed feast."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission draws the distinction between a defilement willed and one not willed, states it in so many words, and applies it to the party whose body produced the defilement. It does not apply it to the officiant, who could no more have prevented the event than the child could, and who is charged a complete baptism. The distinction exists here and is not available to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The excuse is also dated forward rather than waived: the child atones later, when it can speak. What is remitted is the invalidity of the rite, not the debt."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Recorded by E. S. Drower in Iraq and published in 1937, in her chapter on lay life, in an account of infant baptism she describes as a rigorous ordeal during which the baby often dies; the reason given for the child's exemption is quoted by her in English as the priests gave it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), pp. 45-46" .

#
# The watch-houses, and what is handed over at them
#

pmc:MandaeanMatarata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "The watch-houses on the way up"@en, "Die Wachthäuser auf dem Weg nach oben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The stations the soul passes between the earthly world and the world of light, each in the charge of a named keeper, and each holding fast a named class of the dead: at one the shedders of blood, at another those who fornicated and stole and swore falsely, and so on. The soul reports the journey in the first person, asks at each station whose watch-house it is and who is bound in it, is answered, and passes."@en ;
    skos:note "The piece is a first-person itinerary and not a doctrine about an afterlife, which is why it is entered as a node: it states what is required at each station and reports the outcome of meeting the requirement, and it does so in a fixed formula repeated station by station."@en ;
    skos:note "The souls held there are said to want a second death and not to get it. The transmission thereby closes off, for the class it is most severe about, the one outcome that would end the matter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 5, piece 3 (text 180,11-184,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā: Der Schatz oder das große Buch der Mandäer (Göttingen: Vandenhoeck und Ruprecht, 1925), pp. 183-186" .

pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "Hand over thy name and thy sign"@en, "Übergib uns deinen Namen und dein Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "What is demanded at each watch-house and what is given. The keepers say: thou man, hand over to us thy name and thy sign, which thou hast fetched out of the waves of the water, out of the treasures of radiance and out of the great baptism of light. The soul names its name and its sign, and the keeper bows deeply and lets it pass."@en ;
    skos:note "What is handed over is not the keeper's name but the soul's own, and it is identified in the text by where it came from: the baptism. The pass at every station is therefore a rite performed while alive, and the itinerary is a statement about the efficacy of that rite made in the form of a journey."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth setting beside the Egyptian gate material this holding already carries, and not by way of a convergence, which this run did not establish and which the disputed contact route would not support in any case. The difference is the point: there the traveller must know the names of the gatekeepers, here he must produce his own. The first is knowledge of a text and can be lost by forgetting; the second is a state conferred by a ceremony and, as pmc:MandaeanAblatioSacramentorum shows, can be taken away."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 5, piece 3 (text 181,8-184,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 184-186" .

#
# The two places where the scripture denies that having it is enough
#

pmc:MandaeanScientiaSineDignitate a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "Understanding the Treasure brings them no benefit"@en, "Das Verständnis des Schatzes bringt ihnen keinen Nutzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The warning addressed to the transmission's own priestly class. The Nasoraeans who are not worthy of the House of Life have committed grave sin, their body stood in transgression and there was defect and blemish in them; and though they understand the Treasure in high degree from the earth, it brings them no benefit; when they arrive at the great gate of the house of Abathur they will be driven back from it and will not behold the place of light."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that makes the transmission interesting to a holding built around the difference between a claim and its warrant. A corpus that puts its whole operative content into books, and requires its priests to know those books by heart, states in one of them that knowing them confers nothing by itself. The condition it adds is worthiness, which is not observable and not countable, and which the same corpus nowhere makes into a test."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also a counter-case to the first clause of pmc:TheTextAndWhatIsWithheld, which says that a transmission putting its operative content into an obtainable text states the condition of attainment as knowing the text and names no further condition a possessor of the text could fail to meet. Here a further condition is named, in the text, against the possessors. Entered as a node and not worked into that generalization by this run, because a counter-case belongs in a protocol and not in the statement it bears on; the protocol is the work of a later run and the address is this node."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator marks the sentence as uncertain in his footnote and says that the word he renders as the Treasure appears here already to mean a collection of religious writings. The node keeps his doubt rather than quoting past it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,1-21), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 323" .

pmc:MandaeanAblatioSacramentorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "The prayers, the masiqta and the baptism are taken away from them"@en, "Gebete, Totenmesse und Taufe werden ihnen abgenommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "What follows in the same piece for the unworthy priest. Driven back from the gate of Abathur, he will not behold the knowledge; it will be taken from him, and he will sink down to the nature of the place of darkness. The hidden prayers he understands from the world will be taken from him, and the hidden prayer-orders, and the well-appointed ships; the masiqta and the baptism will be taken from him, and these will ascend to the place from which they came; the hidden treasure will be taken from him and placed with the great Jur, the treasurer."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite is treated as a possession that can be repossessed, and the text says where it goes: back up, to where it came from. Read together with pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum this closes a circle inside the corpus. The pass at the watch-houses is the baptism; the baptism can be taken away; and the man it is taken from is turned back at the gate that all the stations lead to."@en ;
    skos:note "No sign accompanies the removal and none is asked for. Nothing in the passage tells the man, or anyone watching him, that his baptism has been taken; the loss is established at the gate and nowhere earlier."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 7 (text 319,21-320,16), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 323-324" .

#
# The cautions
#

pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "Learn not the sorcery of Satan"@en, "Lernet nicht des Satans Zauberwerk"@de ;
    skos:definition "The prohibition standing in the commandments of the first book and repeated in the parallel recension of the second: learn not the sorcery of Satan and bear no witness of falsehood; every one who perverts the law is consumed by the blazing fire. The neighbouring commandment warns that the song of Satan is full of sorcery, deception and seduction, and perverts the mind of the perfect."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanNomenEtSignum ;
    skos:note "Entered with the fact that stands against it, because the fact is in the same book that carries the prohibition and was recorded by the same person who transcribed the practice. The recorder states that many Mandaean priests, in spite of the Ginzā's prohibition of such practices, derive part of their income from writing amulets and from sorcery when their lawful fees are insufficient. The corpus of lead rolls and incantation bowls in Mandaic therefore stands in a field its own scripture condemns, and a reader who takes either the prohibition or the practice alone has half of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is printed twice in the Ginzā in two recensions of the same commandment list, once numbered ninety-seven in the first book and once thirty-two in the second, and the two differ in wording but not in the clause. That is a repetition inside one work and not a second witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 1, commandment 97 (text 13,11-14,20), and book 2, first piece, commandment 32 (text 35,3-36,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 16 and 35" .

pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusDivinos a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "Go not to the soothsayers and the Chaldeans of falsehood"@en, "Gehet nicht zu den Wahrsagern und Chaldäern der Lüge"@de ;
    skos:definition "The commandment forbidding recourse to diviners: go not to the soothsayers and the Chaldeans of falsehood, for they will be lodged in the darkness. The parallel recension adds that the soothsayers and Chaldeans themselves will be lodged in the darkness, so that the ground given is their end and not the falsity of what they say."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MandaeanCautioAdversusMagiam ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is not that divination fails. Neither recension says the soothsayers are wrong; both say where they will end up. A transmission that wanted to make its own procedures the only working ones had an easy sentence available here and did not use it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 1, commandment 143 (text 19,25-21,3), and book 2, first piece, commandment 55 (text 37,16-38,22), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), pp. 22 and 37" .

pmc:MandaeanPoenaPatris a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba ;
    rdfs:label "The daughter goes to the soothsayers and the father is called to account"@en, "Die Tochter geht zu den Wahrsagern, und der Vater wird zur Rechenschaft gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause in the fifteenth book on the daughter who has separated herself from the Nasoraean way: if she prepares offerings for the temples, judgement will be passed upon her father; if she goes to the soothsayers and Chaldeans, he will be called to account at the throne of the raging Nerig."@en ;
    skos:note "The fault is hers and the reckoning is his, and the passage says so twice in two lines with two different faults. It is the plainest case of a displaced penalty this run found, and it is displaced in the direction the holding already knows from the Vedic material, from the transmitting party to the party who did not act."@en ;
    skos:note "The same piece puts the same structure the other way round a few lines further on, where each man who smells the odour of the soothsayers will be made to smell the odour of decay. There the penalty stays with the one who acted. The transmission carries both and does not say what decides which."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 15, piece 1 (text 301,15-302,8), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 299" .

#
# The one place where the works read here contradict each other
#

pmc:MandaeanSacerdotissaeDisputatae a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GinzaRabba, pmw:DrowerMandaeansOfIraqAndIran ;
    rdfs:label "Whether this transmission consecrated women"@en, "Ob diese Überlieferung Frauen geweiht hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three statements that cannot all be taken at face value. The priestly manual says, in the words the field record quotes: beware lest ye consecrate a woman as priest. The field record states that sex is not a bar, that there have been women priests and high priestesses, that a woman of the high rank may perform only one kind of marriage, and that the names of such women occur in the ancestor lists; and in the same paragraph that the recorder never met one or heard of one existing, but was assured there had been some in the past. And the thirteenth book of the right Ginzā, which the field record cites as mentioning Mandaean priestesses, has at that place a feminine plural which its translator renders as women disciples and refuses in a footnote to render as priestesses, on the ground that there are none among the Mandaeans."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore ;
    skos:note "The reason this is a node and not a remark is the translator's footnote, which was read at the wording in this run. The ground he gives for excluding the reading is the present state of the community; and the present state of the community is precisely what the passage would bear on if the other reading were right. A textual question is being decided by an appeal to the fact in question, in a work that is the standard translation and is cited by everything downstream of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition in the manual points the same way as the disputed reading and not away from it. A rule forbidding an act is not usually made against an impossibility, and the recorder quotes this one immediately after reporting that she was assured there had been such women. What none of the three gives is a case: no consecration of a woman is described anywhere in the works read here."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered against pmc:MandaeanSacerdosIntegerCorpore because that is the node carrying the manual's conditions on who may be consecrated, and the dispute is about the extent of those conditions. The manual could not be obtained, so both of its sentences in this file stand at second hand through the same intermediary, which is a limit on the whole dispute and not only on one side of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mandaean ;
    dcterms:source "Ginzā, right side, book 13 (text 287,10-288,24), in the German of Mark Lidzbarski, Ginzā (Göttingen 1925), p. 285 with his footnote 1" ,
                   "E. S. Drower, The Mandaeans of Iraq and Iran (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1937), p. 147" .
